Date: Tue, 26 Jan 1993 15:49:55 -0500 (EST)
From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.UU.NET (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

> > I respect anyone's choice to be a vegetarian.  However, I also feel a need
> > to point out, quietly, that this path is not for everyone.  The energy
> > density of vegetables is much lower than animal products.  If you live in a
> > very cold climate, vegetarianism can almost literally kill you.  Even in
> > our climate, I know people who tried going vegan and had to stop when they
> > realized they were slowly starving to death.
> 
> I'm sorry, Eric, but this is silly and not borne out by the evidence.

What kind of evidence would you consider sufficient?  I've got lots.  And your
focus on B12 is too narrow; there are other things very difficult to get
from a vegan diet.  Zinc, for example.

> If you need more calories, you can just eat more. Most people's bodies are
> structured to give them very clear signals that they aren't eating enough.

Think so?  In fact, many peoples' hunger/satiety signals are quite unreliable;
that's why so many people have overating/undereating problems.

> > Human beings are not really designed to be vegetarians --- our dentition,
> > digestion, and dietary requirements are those of omnivores.
> 
> Quite false, actually. True omnivores like Bears have much shorter digestive
> tracts and teeth designed for eating meat. Humans CAN digest meat, but
> we don't seem to be built for a true omnivorous diet. Our gut length, teeth
> and other factors seem to mark us out as creatures primarily designed
> for a vegetarian diet with the capacity to eat meat when it is available.

I stand by my previous statement.  Human dentition is particularly telling;
it includes teeth specialized for tearing and macerating meat.  This is not
a trivial phenotypic investment.  Compare it with mammals of omnivorous habit,
then with herbivores.  Just as telling is the fossil evidence from Man's
ancestors; interestingly, most had *fewer* adaptations for meat-eating than we
do.  And, on top of all this, there's behavior.  Any theory that claims human
beings are natural vegetarians has to deal with the fact that almost all humans
on what you'd call a "natural" diet experience intense meat cravings --- in
fact, there are hunter-gatherer cultures that have a separate basic word for
 "meat hunger".
 
>                 There are cultures around the world that live on nearly
> vegan diets and are perfectly healthy.

I used to believe this myself, but am now convinced it's a myth (or, more
charitably, that it depends on a loose reading of "nearly").  A vegetarian
anthropologist friend of mine says the normal human pattern seems to include
eating a lot of meat on "feast days".
 
> I'd say its the meat eaters that have to be careful -- and I'd also say
> that the technological help required to manage having your coronary arteries
> completely occluded by athrosclerotic plaques is far greater than that
> required to survive on my diet.

Bzzt.  Recent studies show that in somewhere between 2/3 and 3/4 of the
population, intake of animal fat has *absolutely no* effect on serum
cholesterol and lipoprotein levels.  This hasn't made it to the diet books yet.
 
>> (Me?  Happy omnivore --- I live, effectively, on raw vegetables and (cooked)
>> meat.  My own version of dietary virtue is avoiding heavily processed foods.
>> I've tried cutting my meat intake as an experiment; it made me hungry all the
>> time.
>
> Why didn't you just eat more?

I did.  It didn't work.  I still felt hungry all the time, and had spells
of lightheadedness.  Often I had the weird experience that I felt hungry
while my stomach was still distended from the last helping of veggies.  It
was extremely unpleasant, and turned me permanently skeptical about most of
today's diet wisdom.
-- 
